    "https://apply.workable.com/north-park-university/j/F7EE3315F4/": {
      "title": "Faculty - Professor of Accounting (Open Rank)",
      "description": "Faculty - Professor of Accounting (Open Rank). School of Business and Nonprofit About North Park Founded in 1891, North Park University is a Christian university affiliated with the Evangelical Covenant Church of over 3,200 students from around the country and the world. Located for over 125 years on the land of the Miami and Potawatomi tribes in what is today Chicago’s northside, NPU has recently been designated a Hispanic Serving Institution and is committed to serving diverse populations. Elevating North Park University’s core distinctives of Christian, city-centered, and intercultural, NPU values diversity among its faculty and is committed to building a racially and culturally diverse intellectual community, and strongly encourages the nomination and candidacies of persons who are Black, Indigenous, and People of Color regardless of gender. North Park University is located in Albany Park neighborhood of Chicago, one of the most ethnically diverse zip codes in the United States. It is nine miles from downtown Chicago. The North Park campus has often been referred to as an oasis in the heart of the city – a place where students from urban, rural, and suburban backgrounds alike can call home. And all of this within the major metropolitan backdrop of Chicago: one of the world’s largest and most diversified economies, renowned for its museums and music, and voted best large city in the U.S. for four years in a row by Condé Nast Traveler. Mission The mission of North Park University is to prepare students for lives of significance and service through education in the liberal arts, professional studies, and theology. Vision Building on our core institutional identity—Christian, city-centered, intercultural—our vision is to create a university of uncommon character and enduring excellence, where faith, learning, and service meet. North Park University seeks applications for a full-time Faculty in Accounting in the School of Business and Nonprofit Management beginning in Fall 2025. The candidate will be responsible to teach at the undergraduate and graduate levels, participate in professional development, and provide service to the university community. Rank is dependent upon qualifications. Position Summary North Park University seeks applications for a full-time faculty position in Accounting in the School of Business and Nonprofit Management beginning in Fall 2025. The candidate will be responsible to teach at the undergraduate and graduate levels, participate in professional development, and provide service to the university community. Rank is dependent upon qualifications. Salary range for this position is $66,000 -$77,700 Master’s degree, in accounting, or related field. Extensive accounting experience. A CPA or CMA certification in the field of accounting. Demonstrated excellence in teaching and active involvement in professional or academic organizations. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Complete the online application and attach all required documents. Medical Insurance Dental Insurance Prescription Plan Flexible Spending Account Commuter Benefit Life Insurance Disability Insurance Retirement Plan Tuition Remission Helwig Recreation Center"
    },
    "https://apply.workable.com/north-park-university/j/EBD1F31CCE/": {
      "title": "Office Manager (Swedish-American Historical Society)",
      "description": "Office Manager (Swedish-American Historical Society). College of Arts and Sciences Position Summary The office manager is responsible for the day-to-day operations of the Society, in consultation with the board chair and the treasurer, Main tasks include handling correspondence (print and email), managing the membership database and renewals, financial revenue, mailings, Mailchimp updates, social media posts, and web-based technology. The office manager is the personal face of the Society to members, potential members, persons contacting the Society for a variety of reasons, and at public events. Reports to: Executive Committee (Board Chair and Treasurer) of the Swedish-American Historical Society; employed through North Park University. About the Society Established in 1948, the Swedish-American Historical Society https://www.swedishamericanhist.org/ is a non-profit organization centrally managed from its office on the campus of North Park University on Chicago’s north side. Members (across the United States and internationally) support the Society’s mission of studying Swedish emigration, the history and culture of Swedish immigrants and their descendants in North America, and the ongoing history of relations between the United States and Sweden, through research, publications, public programs, and archives. Hours and Location: Up to 18 hours weekly based on current Society projects and events, averaging 12 hours weekly, On-site at North Park University 60%. Remote 40%. Occasional duties at special public events on evenings and weekends. Essential Responsibilities Promptly receive and respond to all inquiries, including mail, email, and social media messages. Serve as members’ and the public’s first point of contact, promptly responding to inquiries and forwarding needs and concerns to the appropriate officer or committee. Manage Society membership database, mailing lists, renewals, and correspondence. Responsible for membership mailings, including packaging and mailing books. Process book and event sales. Process revenue and incoming donations using QuickBooks and drop-off bank deposits. Track donations and send acknowledgment letters. Update content on the website as needed, post on social media accounts, and send updates via MailChimp on a regular schedule in consultation with the board chair, treasurer, and Communications and Membership Committee. Maintain print and digital files and prepare records to transfer to the archives. Assist with meetings and special events in coordination with the Program Committee. Assist with meetings and production of newsletter Glimtar and other news announcements in coordination with the Communications and Membership Committee. Assist committees and board in scheduling and arranging video meetings. Organize and maintain office operations. Other office duties may be assigned. Salary: $20 per hour Essential Qualifications Facility with software applications: MS Office Suite; Google drive and email; ACT! membership database (or similar platform); and Zoom meeting software. Proven office management, administrative or assistant experience Attention to detail and accuracy. Self-motivated. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Strong organizational and planning skills. Excellent time management skills and ability to multitask and prioritize work. Committed to building strong working relationships and a positive work environment. Desired Qualifications/Skills/Experience WordPress website editing skills or willingness to be trained. QuickBooks, or willingness to be trained. MailChimp or similar messaging service."
    },
    "https://apply.workable.com/north-park-university/j/729007054C/": {
      "title": "Graduate Assistant : Women's Volleyball Coach",
      "description": "Graduate Assistant : Women's Volleyball Coach. Athletics North Park University, a member of the College Conference of Illinois and Wisconsin, would like to announce that applications are now being accepted for the position of Women’s Volleyball Graduate Assistant Coach. The assistantship will begin in January 2027 and includes tuition remission, a partial meal plan, and a $3,000 stipend per semester. About North Park Founded in 1891, North Park University is a Christian university affiliated with the Evangelical Covenant Church of over 3,200 students from around the country and the world. Located for over 125 years on the land of the Miami and Potawatomi tribes in what is today Chicago’s northside, NPU has recently been designated a Hispanic Serving Institution and is committed to serving diverse populations. Elevating North Park University’s core distinctives of Christian, city-centered, and intercultural, NPU values diversity among its faculty and is committed to building a racially and culturally diverse intellectual community, and strongly encourages the nomination and candidacies of persons who are Black, Indigenous, and People of Color regardless of gender. North Park University is located in the Albany Park neighborhood of Chicago, one of the most ethnically diverse zip codes in the United States. It is nine miles from downtown Chicago. The North Park campus has often been referred to as an oasis in the heart of the city – a place where students from urban, rural, and suburban backgrounds alike can call home. And all of this within the major metropolitan backdrop of Chicago: one of the world’s largest and most diversified economies, renowned for its museums and music, and voted best large city in the U.S. for four years in a row by Condé Nast Traveler. Mission The mission of North Park University is to prepare students for lives of significance and service through education in the liberal arts, professional studies, and theology. Vision Building on our core institutional identity—Christian, city-centered, intercultural—our vision is to create a university of uncommon character and enduring excellence, where faith, learning, and service meet. Duties And Responsibilities Assists the head coach with the daily operations of the women’s volleyball program at North Park University. Analyze performance and instruct student-athletes in volleyball techniques and strategies in preparation for and during competition. Assist with all phases of competitive NCAA and CCIW compliant recruiting. Execute practice and match planning/evaluation, including video exchange and breakdown. Ensure a commitment to the academic success of volleyball student-athletes, including monitoring academic performance, attendance, and social behavior. Build and sustain positive relationships with student-athletes, parents, alumni, faculty, staff, and the campus community. Manage equipment usage. Complete other duties as assigned by the Head Women’s Volleyball Coach, Assistant Vice President for Athletics, and Associate and Assistant Athletic Directors. Candidates must possess a bachelor’s degree and obtain acceptance into a North Park University graduate program. Coaching and/or playing experience and thorough knowledge of collegiate volleyball are required. Candidates must be able to work an untraditional work schedule, including frequent evenings and weekends, and lift and move athletic equipment. A valid driver’s license is required, and candidate must be able to travel with the team by vehicle or plane. Candidates must possess a clear understanding and personal commitment to North Park’s mission of Christian higher education."
    },
    "https://apply.workable.com/north-park-university/j/DDE588043C/": {
      "title": "Benefits Specialist",
      "description": "Benefits Specialist. Human Resources About North Park Founded in 1891, North Park University is a Christian university affiliated with the Evangelical Covenant Church. Located in Chicago, North Park serves students from across the country and around the world through education in the liberal arts, professional studies, and theology. Mission The mission of North Park University is to prepare students for lives of significance and service through education in the liberal arts, professional studies, and theology. Vision Building on our core institutional identity of being Christian, city-centered, and intercultural, our vision is to create a university of uncommon character and enduring excellence, where faith, learning, and service meet. Position Summary The Benefits Specialist administers the University's employee benefit, retirement, and leave programs. The position manages benefit enrollments, employee education, vendor and payroll coordination, billing reconciliations, required notices and reporting, plan audits, and benefits records. The Benefits Specialist serves as a primary resource for employees with benefit and leave questions and ensures that transactions are timely, accurate, confidential, and consistent with plan terms and applicable requirements. Duties and Responsibilities Benefits Enrollment and Employee Support Administer medical, dental, vision, life, disability, flexible spending, dependent care, health savings, parking, transit, and other assigned employee benefit programs. Guide new employees through benefit enrollment, respond to questions, monitor enrollment completion, and process elections accurately in the benefits enrollment platform and Ellucian Colleague. Process qualifying life event changes, coverage terminations, dependent eligibility changes, beneficiary updates, and other approved benefit transactions. Plan and administer annual open enrollment, including system configuration support, employee communications, meetings, benefit guides, deadlines, and completion tracking. Monitor and respond to the benefits inbox and provide clear, timely, and confidential assistance to employees, retirees, and eligible dependents. Maintain accurate benefits content on HR web pages, enrollment materials, guides, forms, and employee resources. Retirement and Vendor Administration Administer the University's retirement plan processes, including enrollment support, contribution changes, participant communications, required disclosures, and coordination with recordkeepers and payroll. Administer the Retiree Benefits Program and maintain accurate retiree eligibility, enrollment, billing, and communication records. Serve as a day-to-day liaison with benefit carriers, retirement providers, administrators, brokers, and other vendors to resolve enrollment, eligibility, claims, billing, and data issues. Reconcile monthly benefit invoices and enrollment records, research discrepancies, secure corrections, and prepare approved invoices for payment. Coordinate with Payroll to validate deductions and funding for insurance, retirement, flexible spending, dependent care, parking, transit, and health savings arrangements. Leave Administration Administer employee leave requests in consultation with the Senior Director, including eligibility review, employee notices, forms, documentation, tracking, and return-to-work coordination. Coordinate Family and Medical Leave Act, short-term disability, long-term disability, pregnancy-related leave, and other applicable leave processes under University policies and governing requirements. Maintain confidential leave files and provide timely information to employees, supervisors, payroll, insurers, and other authorized parties. Notify the Title IX Coordinator of confidential pregnancy-related leave matters when required. Compliance Audits and Records Prepare and process Forms 1095-C and other required benefit-related government documentation and employee notices. Serve as the HR lead for benefit plan audits by researching requests, reconciling system discrepancies, producing documents, and tracking completion. Prepare and distribute required retirement plan disclosures and other assigned plan notices. Maintain accurate and confidential benefit and leave records in enrollment platforms, Ellucian Colleague, and designated document systems. Review benefit processes and records for compliance with plan documents, University policies, and applicable federal, state, and local requirements; promptly elevate concerns to the Senior Director. Monitor regulatory and plan changes relevant to assigned benefits and leave programs and update procedures, communications, and forms as directed. Support benefit renewals, implementation activities, data requests, testing, and other benefits projects assigned by the Senior Director. Salary range: $55,000 - $65,000 Required Bachelor's degree in human resources or a related field. At least two years of experience in benefits administration and human resources in a higher education environment. Experience administering 403(b) retirement plans, ACA reporting, vendor billing, and open enrollment administration. Working knowledge of benefit enrollment systems and HR information systems . Working knowledge of benefit and leave requirements, including the Family and Medical Leave Act, COBRA, HIPAA privacy requirements, and applicable benefits compliance practices. Strong reconciliation and documentation skills. Proficiency with Microsoft Word, Excel, Outlook, PowerPoint, and related productivity tools. Personal commitment to and ability to articulate support for North Park University's mission, identity, and Christian faith commitment. Eligible to work in the United States without sponsorship. Experience with benefits audits. Preferred Spanish-speaking Familiarity with Ellucian Colleague software Working knowledge of Employee Navigator portal CEBS, SHRM-CP, PHR, or another relevant benefits or HR certification. Salary range: $55,000 to $65,000 Medical Insurance Dental Insurance Prescription Plan Flexible Spending Account Commuter Benefit Life Insurance Disability Insurance Retirement Plan Tuition Remission Helwig Recreation Center"
    },
    "https://apply.workable.com/north-park-university/j/3CA2D614B6/": {
      "title": "Student Success Specialist",
      "description": "Student Success Specialist. Student Engagement About North Park Founded in 1891, North Park University is a Christian university affiliated with the Evangelical Covenant Church of over 3,200 students from around the country and the world. North Park University is located in the Albany Park neighborhood of Chicago, one of the most ethnically diverse zip codes in the United States. It is nine miles from downtown Chicago. North Park has recently been designated a Hispanic Serving Institution and is committed to serving diverse populations. Elevating North Park University’s core distinctives of Christian, city centered, and intercultural, NPU values diversity among its faculty and is committed to building a racially and culturally diverse intellectual community and strongly encourages the nomination and candidacies of persons who are Black, Indigenous, People of Color regardless of gender. The North Park campus has often been referred to as an oasis in the heart of the city – a place where students from urban, rural, and suburban backgrounds alike can call home. And all of this within the major metropolitan backdrop of Chicago: one of the world’s largest and most diversified economies, renowned for its museums and music, and voted best large city in the U.S. for four years in a row by Condé Nast Traveler. Mission The mission of North Park University is to prepare students for lives of significance and service through education in the liberal arts, professional studies, and theology. Vision Building on our core institutional identity—Christian, city-centered, intercultural—our vision is to create a university of uncommon character and enduring excellence, where faith, learning, and service meet. Position Summary The Student Success Specialist assists in the development, implementation, and assessment of student success initiatives designed to support students through key academic transitions. This role fosters cross-campus collaboration, utilizes data-informed practices to improve student engagement, retention, and persistence, and contributes to the development of programs, services, and resources that promote student success. This position creates inclusive opportunities for students to build community, connect with campus resources, develop leadership skills, and successfully navigate their academic journey toward graduation. Duties and Responsibilities Coordinate and facilitate student success programs, workshops, trainings, and engagement initiatives that support students through critical transition periods throughout the student life cycle. Collaborate with faculty, staff, and campus partners to provide holistic, wraparound support services that promote student engagement, academic success, belonging, retention, and persistence. Oversee the recruitment, hiring, training, supervision, development, and evaluation of student leaders and other peer mentor programs that support student success initiatives. Support the continued development, implementation, and expansion of the mentorship program through outreach, training, assessment, and strategic program growth aligned with institutional retention goals. Develop, maintain, and promote online and in-person student success resources, toolkits, and educational materials for students, faculty, staff, and families. Collect, analyze, and interpret program assessment data and key performance indicators to evaluate program effectiveness and make recommendations for continuous improvement. Collaborate in the planning and implementation of new student onboarding, transition, and orientation programs in partnership with campus stakeholders. Teach or facilitate seminars, workshops, and presentations focused on academic success strategies, campus resource utilization, leadership development, and student transition support. Support special projects, strategic initiatives, and university-wide retention efforts related to student success, engagement, persistence, and graduation outcomes. Perform other duties as assigned by the Director of First Year Experience to advance student success, retention, persistence, and institutional strategic priorities. Salary: $52,000 per year Bachelor's degree required, Master’s Degree in higher education or related field preferred. Ability to work effectively in a diverse and team-oriented work environment. Competency in working with diversity along multiple dimensions: first-generation. college students, race, ethnicity, immigration status, gender, sexuality, and ability status. Strong written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to reach multiple audiences, i.e., administrators, faculty, staff, students, and parents. Excellent problem solving, time, and project management skills. Ability to prioritize projects and tasks and assess and deploy resources. Experience with assessment and best practices in student development/student affairs. Prior experience working and collaborating with student leaders/ stakeholders. Appreciation for, personal commitment to, and ability to effectively articulate North Park’s mission to Christian higher education. Medical Insurance Dental Insurance Prescription Plan Flexible Spending Account Commuter Benefit Life Insurance Disability Insurance Retirement Plan Tuition Remission Helwig Recreation Center"
    }
  },
